---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeRadixText from '@/components/tools/dev/DevEncodeRadixText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="base32-encoder-decoder">
  <DevEncodeRadixText client:load radix="base32" direction="encode" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What Base32 is for</h2>
    <p>
      Base32 encodes binary data with 32 characters: A–Z and 2–7. That is five bits per character,
      against six for Base64, so the output is 60% larger, not 33%. In exchange, the alphabet is
      case-insensitive, has no characters that look alike, and you can speak, handwrite or type it
      from a screen without errors.
    </p>

    <h2>Why 0, 1 and 8 are missing</h2>
    <p>
      They were removed on purpose. Zero and capital O look the same in many fonts; one, capital I and
      lowercase L are worse; eight and capital B are similar on a low-resolution display. Without those
      digits, the usual transcription errors cannot occur. For this reason, two-factor authentication
      secrets use Base32 and not Base64.
    </p>

    <h2>Two alphabets</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>RFC 4648 standard</th><th>base32hex (§7)</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Alphabet</td><td>A–Z then 2–7</td><td>0–9 then A–V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sorts in byte order</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Used by</td><td>TOTP secrets, Tor v3 addresses, some S3 keys</td><td>DNSSEC NSEC3 records</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Value of the character A</td><td>0</td><td>10</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The two give completely different output for the same input, so decoding with the wrong one gives
      data that looks plausible but is wrong, with no error. If a decode is nonsense, try the other
      alphabet before you assume the data is corrupt.
    </p>

    <h2>Padding</h2>
    <p>
      Base32 works in 40-bit groups: five bytes in, eight characters out. When the input does not
      divide evenly, the RFC pads to a multiple of eight with <code>=</code>. For this reason you see
      one, three, four or six equals signs, but never two, five or seven. Most implementations accept
      unpadded input, and TOTP secrets are almost always shared without padding. This decoder ignores
      padding.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading a TOTP secret</h2>
    <p>
      When an authenticator app shows a secret such as <code>JBSW Y3DP EHPK 3PXP</code>, that is Base32
      with a space after every four characters for legibility. Decode it here to get the raw key bytes:
      10 bytes for that example, 20 for the 160-bit secret that the RFC recommends. The decoder accepts
      spaces and lowercase, so paste the secret as shown. To see the codes that a secret produces, use
      the TOTP generator on this site, which also works offline.
    </p>

    <h2>Size comparison</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Encoding</th><th>Bits per character</th><th>Growth</th><th>Case-sensitive</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hex</td><td>4</td><td>+100%</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Base32</td><td>5</td><td>+60%</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Base64</td><td>6</td><td>+33%</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Use hex when a person must read individual bytes, Base32 when a person must copy the whole value,
      and Base64 when nobody reads it and size matters.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
